## 9.  Quick Reference Code

```java
public class FileProcessor {

    // Checked
    public void process(String path) throws FileProcessingException {
        try {
            readAndValidate(path);
        } catch (IOException e) {
            // Wrap checked into domain exception
            throw new FileProcessingException("I/O failure", e);
        } catch (InvalidFormatException e) {
            // Unchecked but declared
            throw new FileProcessingException("Invalid format", e);
        }
    }

    private void readAndValidate(String path) throws IOException, InvalidFormatException {
        try (BufferedReader br = new BufferedReader(new FileReader(path))) {
            // read & validate
            if (!validate(br)) throw new InvalidFormatException("Bad header");
        }
    }

    private boolean validate(BufferedReader br) {
        // might throw unchecked exceptions if input is null, etc.
        return true;
    }
}
```

---

## 10.  Summary

| Aspect | Checked Exception | Unchecked Exception |
|--------|-------------------|---------------------|
| Declaration | Must `throw` or `catch` | No requirement |
| Typical use | Recoverable / anticipated failures | Programming errors, illegal states |
| Propagation | Forces callers to handle | Caller can ignore |
| Example | `IOException`, `SQLException` | `NullPointerException`, `IllegalArgumentException` |
| Best practice | Use for *recoverable* conditions; keep hierarchy shallow | Use for *bugs*; prefer `RuntimeException` subclasses |
| Handling | `try/catch/throws` | `try/catch` if you wish, otherwise ignore |
| Resource cleanup | `finally` or `try‑with‑resources` | Same, but not required |

> **Bottom line:**  
> *Treat checked exceptions as a contract for the caller that something might fail that the caller can recover from.*  
> *Treat unchecked exceptions as a signal that the API was misused; they should not be forced to be handled.*  

Use the patterns above, keep the `try` block minimal, catch specific exceptions, and always document why you choose one approach over the other. Happy coding!
